  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Emergency response for drought affected communities and IDPs in Borena zone</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Better access to safe water and sanitation facilities, and improved hygiene practices through the provision of water rationing, WASH infrastructure, emergency WASH NFIs and hygiene promotion sessions for 65,183 (34,419 female &amp; 30,764 male, including 15,344 older persons and 8,393 PwDs) people.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved food security of 1,800 (800 male and 1,000 females, including 1,250 older persons and 50 PwDs) at risk households (those led by older persons and other vulnerable community groups) through multipurpose cash-based response, nutrition assessment &amp; referral.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The prolonged drought in Ethiopia has compromised fragile livelihoods of the vulnerable population. According to OCHA&apos;s drought update, June 2022, more than 8 million people are currently in need of food and water assistance . FEWSNET also indicated widespread livestock deaths, and population displacements, which would drive a deterioration of an already dire food security and malnutrition situation in 2023.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">To contribute to improved health outcomes of drought affected communities and IDPs, targeting 66,983 (26,995 men, 4,569 boys, 31185 women and 4,224 girls, including 16,594 older people and 8,443 PwDs) beneficiaries in three targeted woredas (Dire, Eleweya and Teltele) of Borena zone, Oromia Region</narrative>
  </description>
